## The core idea: a chain running inside a partitioning system

Fat loss is a **pipeline** — mobilize → transport → oxidize → eliminate — where each gate is a place a drug acts directly. Muscle building is different in kind. It's a **causal chain with a non-negotiable first link**, and that chain runs *inside* a body-wide **nutrient-partitioning system** that decides where every molecule you eat actually ends up.

```
   THE CHAIN          Stimulus ──► mTORC1 ──► MPS ──► tissue accretion

   THE SYSTEM     food ──► digestion ──► bloodstream ──► oxidize / store / build
   AROUND IT      governed by: training status · insulin · leptin · thyroid
                               · cortisol · androgens · GH/IGF-1
```

You never "turn food into muscle" directly. Food becomes physical molecules in the blood, and the body then decides whether each one is oxidized for ATP, stored as muscle or liver glycogen, stored as adipose triglyceride, spent as amino-acid substrate for repair and MPS, drawn down by organs and brain as their non-negotiable operating budget, or simply converted and excreted. ==So "build muscle" is never just "activate mTOR."== It's six jobs at once: create a reason to build tissue, switch on the growth machinery, supply enough amino acids and energy, hold nitrogen balance positive, steer the surplus toward trained muscle instead of fat, and keep the endocrine environment permissive enough for all of it to keep happening.

> [!important] The off-season rule
> Pharmacology can amplify almost every link — but it cannot replace Link 1. No androgen, GH, peptide, insulin signal, or osmolyte builds meaningful contractile tissue without a mechanical reason to. This is the 90/10 rule from [[Fat Loss Pathways (Theory)|the cutting side]] in different clothes: training and food are the 90%, the chemistry is the margin that makes the 90% pay off faster.

> [!note] Why the off-season feels "less direct" than a cut
> On a cut, a compound *is* the mechanism — Mirabegron *is* the Beta-3 signal. In a bulk, a compound mostly **removes a brake or adds a green light** to a process the *training* started. That's also why results lag the stack by weeks: you're growing capacity — myonuclei, satellite cells, mitochondria, receptor density — not flipping an acute switch.

