## Link 1 — The stimulus: tension, recruitment, performance

**Mechanical tension is the prerequisite.** The muscle has to experience enough force, enough proximity to failure, and enough progressive overload to conclude that the tissue it currently has is not enough for the job. Strip this away and everything downstream is levers attached to nothing.

The stimulus is really two things working together. **Recruitment** brings the high-threshold Type II fibres into play — through heavy loading, hard sets near failure, or enough accumulated fatigue that the larger motor units are forced to fire. **Mechanical strain** then places those recruited fibres under enough tension that the cell converts physical stress into biochemical growth signalling. You need both: recruiting a fibre you don't load does nothing, and loading a fibre you can't recruit is impossible.

Pharmacology and nutrition can't manufacture the stimulus, but they let you *express more of it*:

- **Fuel & performance prep** — carbs, sodium, [[Creatine Monohydrate (1000g) — Shopee|creatine]], [[Glycerol (1000g) — Shopee|glycerol]], [[L-Citrulline (1000g) — Shopee|citrulline]], and hydration fill the sarcoplasm and improve leverage, pump, and session quality. A fuller muscle is a better lever, so this feeds Link 1 directly (and loops back through Governor 2).
- **Neural drive** — caffeine and an androgenic state raise aggression, confidence, and recruitment capacity, letting the brain reach the Type II fibres it can't access when tired or passive. This is a *training-output* effect, not tissue growth in the moment.
- **Recovery capacity** — androgens, the GH-axis tools, sleep, and nutrition let you tolerate more *productive* volume before fatigue wins, which raises the total stimulus you can accumulate across a block.

> [!note] Osmolality already touches Link 1
> A fuller cell isn't only a downstream "pump." Glycogen, water, and electrolytes improve leverage, joint feel, and force output before you've grown anything — so cell volume feeds the *stimulus itself*, then feeds mTORC1 again downstream. Hold that thought; it's the loop in Governor 2.

> [!note] The calcium micro-stimulus
> Tension isn't only an mTORC1 signal. Each contraction releases and reuptakes **calcium** from the sarcoplasmic reticulum, and that Ca²⁺ flux activates a *parallel* pathway — calcineurin and the CaMKs — that drives **mitochondrial biogenesis and fibre-type adaptation**. It's why training builds the *machinery and architecture* (mitochondria, capillaries, fibre profile) to match the contractile protein mTORC1 lays down — growth that keeps the bigger muscle actually functional, not just thicker.

