## Link 2 — mTORC1: the growth switch and its green lights

mTORC1 is the central growth switch. It doesn't fire on one input — it integrates mechanical signals, nutrient availability, hormonal/growth-factor input, and cellular energy status before it lets MPS run hard. The popular line — *"mTOR is purely mechanical, nothing else touches it"* — is too narrow. A better model is a switch with **four green lights**, and an enhanced protocol can deliberately turn on three of them:

```
 Mechanical input ───────────────┐   (external tension + cell-volume stretch)
 Amino acids / leucine ──────────┤
 Growth factors / AAS ───────────┼──► mTORC1 ──► MPS
 Energy status (AMPK low enough) ─┘
```

1. **Mechanical input — tension *and* cell volume.** Link 1's external load is the primary trigger, but it isn't the only *mechanical* signal the cell reads. Cell swelling — glycogen, creatine, sodium/potassium balance, glycerol, and water pulling fluid in — stretches the membrane and cytoskeleton, and the cell senses that stretch through the **same mechanotransduction machinery** as external load. ==A swollen cell is read as a structurally-stressed, well-resourced state, so it responds the only way it can: up-regulate synthesis and down-regulate breakdown to reinforce the enlarged cell.== That's *how* the pump is growth-permissive — not a vague "anabolic feeling" but membrane stretch → mechanosensing → pro-synthesis, anti-catabolic signalling converging on mTORC1. (This is why cell volume belongs here under the mechanical light, and *also* runs as the feed-forward loop in Governor 2.)
2. **Amino-acid availability — leucine.** Leucine and the EAAs signal that raw material is present — the intracellular leucine sensor releases its brake on the pathway. The legitimate OTC lever: ~3 g leucine or a full EAA feed peri-workout via [[Intra-Workout (651g) — iHerb|the intra-workout blend]].
3. **Growth factors & androgens** - the Akt light (where pharmacology plugs in).** This is the green light an enhanced protocol owns, and it reads cleanest as **insulin and androgens grouped into one converging input** (the same way the [[Fat Loss Pathways (Theory)|cutting note]] groups them): both funnel into **PI3K → Akt**, which **suppresses TSC2** — a brake on mTORC1 — so pulling that brake off sits the switch closer to "on," *independent of exercise*.
	1. **Insulin / IGF-1 / GH** drive Akt directly — the fast, potent route (and the dangerous one; see the caution below)
	2. **Androgens** reach the same Akt/mTOR bridge through **IGF-1 cross-talk**, *on top of* their direct work at the androgen receptor — AR-driven transcription, satellite-cell/myonuclear support, and lowered catabolic pressure. So an androgen both opens this green light *and* does jobs the other lights can't.
4. E**nergy sufficiency — AMPK low enough.** AMPK is the cell's low-fuel alarm; when it's high (under-fed, glycogen-empty), it pushes back on mTORC1. Enough energy keeps the alarm quiet — and this is the hinge of concurrent-training interference: too much endurance work holds AMPK up and blunts the growth signal.

> [!caution] Insulin is the sharpest green light — and the easiest to misuse
> Exogenous insulin strongly supports both storage and mTOR signalling, which is exactly why it's the fastest way to gain fat, go hypoglycemic, or worse. It stays behind the hard gate in [[Bulk Recovery & Partitioning Protocol]] — clinician plan, glucose monitoring, carb plan, hypo rescue, explicit reason. For this system the default route is **food-driven insulin plus training-induced GLUT4 translocation**. ==Do not chase mTOR with injected insulin.==

