## Link 3 — MPS, nitrogen, and the anatomy of a built muscle

mTORC1 drives **Muscle Protein Synthesis** — ribosomes stitching amino acids into new protein. But growth is never "MPS high" in isolation; net gain is a balance against breakdown:

```
   net muscle gain  =  MPS  −  muscle protein breakdown
```

**Nitrogen retention is the accounting layer over that balance** — and the link is more literal than it sounds. Protein is the *only* macronutrient that carries nitrogen (carbs and fats don't), and when the body breaks an amino acid down for fuel or disposal, it strips the nitrogen off and excretes it as **urea**. So *nitrogen in* (protein eaten) versus *nitrogen out* (urea excreted) forms a measurable balance sheet — and that balance sheet tracks the otherwise-invisible MPS−MPB process directly: if you're **retaining** nitrogen (in > out), more amino acids are being locked into tissue than are being stripped and excreted, which is just another way of saying synthesis is beating breakdown. You can't watch MPS−MPB hour to hour, but you *can* read its ledger. ==Positive nitrogen balance ≈ net tissue gain; negative ≈ you're losing or wasting substrate.== Androgens shift the ledger hard in your favour — more synthesis, less breakdown, a larger fraction of dietary amino acids "sticking" — while cortisol, illness, a severe deficit, and poor sleep push the other way. Nitrogen retention isn't a fourth tissue compartment; it's the bookkeeping that tells you whether the protein you ate became muscle or got burned and excreted.

When MPS does win, the volume it adds splits into **three distinct compartments** — a "big" muscle is three things expanding at once, not one:

```
                 [ Total skeletal-muscle volume ]
        ┌────────────────┼─────────────────┐
        ▼                ▼                  ▼
 Myofibrillar      Sarcoplasmic       Satellite / myonuclei
 (contractile)     (glycogen+water)   (the genetic ceiling)
```

**A · Myofibrillar — the lean tissue you actually want.** New actin and myosin contractile filaments laid down by MPS. Dense, hard, *permanent* structure that best survives a diet and a dose-drop. This is the real prize; everything else serves it.

**B · Sarcoplasmic — glycogen, water, enzymes, mitochondria.** The fluid and energy substrate around the filaments. Not useless water: a glycogen-stuffed, hydrated sarcoplasm raises intramuscular pressure, which improves leverage and joint stability and directly feeds **strength** — and strength feeds Link 1's stimulus. It fills faster and empties faster than myofibrillar mass.

**C · Satellite cells & myonuclei — the ceiling and the memory.** Satellite cells are muscle stem cells on the fibre's outside; training makes them proliferate and **androgens accelerate it sharply**. They donate their nuclei to existing fibres, and because each nucleus can only service a fixed volume of cytoplasm (the *myonuclear domain*), more nuclei permanently raise the ceiling for how much myofibrillar mass a fibre can hold. Those donated nuclei stay for life — the literal mechanism of muscle memory, and the reason long GH/androgen blocks are an investment in *next* year's ceiling, not just this week's size.

> [!tip] The off-season target
> The best bulk expands all three: **A** for keepable contractile tissue, **B** for performance and fullness, **C** for future capacity. MPS only pulls amino acids and glucose into A and B (rather than into fat) when partitioning is steering traffic there — which is Link 4.

