## Link 4 — Nutrient partitioning: where the matter actually goes

Partitioning is **a layer in its own right** — a chain sitting between the bloodstream and the muscle-building chain above. Before any of mTORC1's green lights matter, the body has already decided how much of what you ate even reaches the muscle. To read it cleanly, separate the **fuel** (the macronutrients) from the **traffic controllers** (the endocrine system — broken down in Link 5 below), and look at the actual physics.

**It's matter being shuttled, not "energy."** Digestion breaks food into physical compounds that enter the blood as a pool of raw material: carbs → **glucose** (plus some fructose/galactose), protein → **amino acids**, fat → **free fatty acids + glycerol**. Partitioning is the set of chemical signals directing those *molecules* to destinations — nothing moves as abstract "energy."

> [!note] The "leftover" misconception
> The body doesn't split a meal into "energy" and "leftovers to partition." It first burns a portion of the incoming molecules immediately for **ATP** — brain, heartbeat, organ function: the non-negotiable BMR tax. Only the molecules *not* oxidized for survival enter the **excess pool** that actually gets partitioned, either stored as reserve fuel (glycogen or fat) or spent as structural building blocks (muscle repair). Brain and organs are not the discretionary pool — the brain alone burns ~20% of daily glucose, stores none, and pulls it from blood continuously (if blood glucose drops, the liver runs gluconeogenesis on amino acids/glycerol to keep it fed). Partitioning only governs what's left **after** that mandatory tax is paid.

```
                 [ Total ingested macronutrients ]
                                │
                  ┌─────────────┴─────────────┐
                  ▼                           ▼
        [ Immediate oxidation ]       [ The excess pool ]
        brain · heart · organs        (what partitioning fights over)
                                              │
                                ┌─────────────┴─────────────┐
                                ▼                           ▼
                         [ Lean tissue ]            [ Adipose tissue ]
```

**"Lean tissue" is not one bucket — glucose and amino acids ride completely separate machinery.** Glycogen replenishment is pure *energy storage*: glucose enters the muscle through **GLUT4** transporters and is packed into glycogen, but there's a hard spatial ceiling (~300–500 g across the body, depending on muscle mass) — once full, surplus glucose is burned off as heat or converted to triglyceride and stored as fat. Muscle protein synthesis is a *structural* process on different rails entirely: amino acids don't touch GLUT4 — they enter through dedicated amino-acid transporters (e.g. **LAT1**), and *if* mTORC1 is lit (Link 2), they're stitched into new actin and myosin to repair and enlarge the fibre. Same "lean" label, two unrelated mechanisms.

Each macro also carries its own partitioning signature, which is why "a calorie" behaves differently depending on its source:

| Macro | Becomes | Partitioning signature |
|---|---|---|
| **Carbohydrate** | glucose | sharp glucose rise → strong **insulin** → GLUT4 translocation → glycogen, or fat once glycogen is full |
| **Protein** | amino acids | mild insulin but strong **mTOR** activation → repair/MPS; excess *can't* be stored as protein — the liver strips the nitrogen → glucose or urea |
| **Fat** | FFA + glycerol | no insulin needed for uptake (**lipoprotein lipase** lets it into cells); but eaten *with* carbs, high insulin makes adipose LPL very active → straight to storage |

> [!tip] GLUT4 is the shipping dock; insulin is the manager; androgens rebuild the warehouse
> Tie it together: **GLUT4 is specifically the loading dock for glucose**, and **insulin** is the manager that orders the docks to open — push glucose into the cell, shut down fat-burning, lock fat in adipose. **Androgens** operate a level up: they rebuild the *architecture* of the warehouse itself — making the muscle store massive, insulin-sensitive, and structurally hungry while shrinking the size and priority of the fat store. And the dock has a second opener that bypasses the manager entirely: ==muscle contraction translocates GLUT4 without insulin== (the AMPK/calcium route), which is why a just-trained muscle is a glucose sink, and why the *same* carb meal partitions completely differently before versus after a hard session. The full controller panel — insulin, leptin, thyroid, cortisol, androgens, GH — is Link 5.

