## Link 5 — The endocrine controllers

Partitioning is run by a panel of signals reading different timescales. The big ones:

| Controller | What it reads / does | Partitioning effect |
|---|---|---|
| **Insulin** | acute incoming carb/protein | drives glucose into muscle/fat, suppresses lipolysis, permits glycogen + MPS |
| **Leptin** | long-term stored energy (mostly fat mass) | tells the brain energy is abundant; sets hunger, thyroid output, metabolic rate |
| **Thyroid** | metabolic throttle | how much fuel is burned vs left available to store/build |
| **Cortisol** | stress / survival pressure | mobilizes amino acids + glucose; chronically high = anti-muscle |
| **Androgens** | AR activation + transcription | raise MPS capacity, nitrogen retention, satellite activity, neural drive, lean bias |
| **GH / IGF-1** | growth, fasting, lipolysis, remodelling | feed IGF-1/satellite/connective tissue — but blunt insulin sensitivity |
| **Training / myokines** | local muscle demand | insulin-independent glucose uptake + local nutrient pull |

**Insulin and leptin run the short and long clocks.** Insulin is the moment-to-moment nutrient manager; leptin is the long-term fuel gauge. High body fat normally means high leptin, but chronically high leptin can tip into **leptin resistance** — the brain stops hearing "we have enough." Chronic overfeeding can blunt insulin sensitivity in parallel, and the ugly end-state is *dual resistance*: a hungry brain, a glucose-resistant muscle, and a body that stores fat easily. This is the real argument against the permanent dirty bulk.

**The "suitable range," not a single number.** The clinical line is linear — *more fat = worse sensitivity* — but partitioning actually has a **window** bounded by two failure modes, and your individual **P-ratio** (the genetic split of a surplus between muscle and fat) is only optimised inside it. Too lean — below your genetic **leptin floor** — and the body reads starvation: T3 drops, cortisol rises, free testosterone dampens, and ==a surplus gets aggressively redirected to restore fat reserves rather than build expensive muscle.== Too fat — past your individual **adipocyte distension ceiling** — and it's fat-cell *dysfunction*, not fat itself: cells expanding by hypertrophy outrun their capillary supply, go hypoxic, and spill inflammatory cytokines (TNF-α, IL-6, MCP-1) that degrade muscle insulin signalling — GLUT4 stops welcoming carbs (this is the Governor 3 inflammation gate, reached from the *inside*).

| Body-fat state | Endocrine status | Surplus lands in |
|---|---|---|
| **Too lean** (below floor) | low leptin · low T3 · high cortisol · suppressed AR | **adipose** — survival restoration |
| **Suitable range** (sweet spot) | robust leptin · optimal thyroid · high androgens · low basal inflammation | **skeletal muscle** — permissive growth |
| **Too fat** (above ceiling) | leptin resistance · high cytokines · insulin resistance | **adipose + ectopic** — muscle goes deaf |

> [!tip] Bulk by oscillating, not by holding a number
> Start a bulk at the **lower end** of your suitable range, where insulin sensitivity is pristine, and ride it up as performance climbs. The moment waist expands disproportionately to your lifts — *or* appetite vanishes and waking glucose drifts — you've hit your distension ceiling: stop the surplus and run a sharp mini-cut back to the floor to reset the system. The target isn't a static body-fat %, it's staying inside the window.

> [!tip] How a bodybuilder keeps insulin sensitivity high
> Mostly unglamorous, mostly free: stay lean enough to bulk, carry more (and higher-quality) muscle, train hard, walk after meals, place carbs around training, sleep enough, keep chronic stress down, hold calories controlled, eat adequate fibre and protein, avoid long uncontrolled surpluses, and mini-cut when the bulk drifts. A compressed eating window helps mainly by lowering total time at elevated insulin — useful, not magic, and only when it improves calories, structure, and adherence overall.

**The GH / MK-677 glucose tax.** The GH-axis tools genuinely support recovery, IGF-1, connective tissue, and long-horizon capacity — and they genuinely worsen glucose control. The mechanism is a chain: [[HGH (100iu-kit) — TCI|GH]] raises lipolysis and circulating free fatty acids → more fatty acids make muscle less willing to burn glucose → GH also interferes with insulin signalling downstream so GLUT4 translocation gets less efficient → the pancreas compensates with more insulin → if it persists, fasting and post-meal glucose drift up. That's precisely why [[Bulk Recovery & Partitioning Protocol]] and [[Glucose & Insulin Sensitivity Protocol]] sit right next to every GH/[[MK-677 (10mg×50tab) — kohohpharma|MK-677]] decision.

> [!warning] Metformin and SGLT2 are glucose-risk tools, not muscle builders
> [[Metformin (1000mg×100tab) — Jigar Healthcare|Metformin]] mainly cuts hepatic glucose output and supports insulin-sensitivity signalling via AMPK; SGLT2 inhibitors dump glucose through the urine. Neither is a "cheat-meal blocker" — they're medical glucose-lowering agents carrying dehydration, infection, and ketoacidosis risk. SGLT2 + low-carb + dehydration + illness + diuretics is a dangerous stack; all of it is governed by [[Glucose & Insulin Sensitivity Protocol]].

> [!note] Deep sleep is a GH/IGF-1 lever, not just cortisol control
> Sleep belongs in this section, not only in monitoring. **Slow-wave (deep) sleep** is the specific window where the pituitary fires the bulk of the night's endogenous GH pulses and the glymphatic system clears metabolic waste. Wreck that architecture and you don't merely raise cortisol — you blunt the overnight liver expression of **IGF-1**, cutting the endocrine arm of green light 3 at its source. Late stimulants, alcohol, and a too-warm room cost you growth, not just rest.

