## Governor 3 — The environmental gates (gut & inflammation)

The whole model so far assumes *ingested matter* cleanly becomes *bloodstream molecules*, and that the muscle can *hear* the growth signals reaching it. Two hidden gates can break both assumptions, and when they fail the chain stalls no matter how high the androgen or the stimulus.

**The gut bottleneck — the rate-limiter on substrate.** In a real bulk the GI tract is usually the *first* system to break. Chronic hyperphagia — especially heavy fats and processed carbs — shifts the microbiome toward dysbiosis, weakens the mucosal barrier, and leaks bacterial endotoxin (LPS) into circulation (low-grade **endotoxemia**). That endotoxin lights a systemic inflammatory response that ==degrades peripheral insulin sensitivity from the gut end== — substrate is absorbed less efficiently, and what does get in is biased toward fat. Your absorption, not your appetite, becomes the ceiling on how much surplus is actually usable.

**The inflammation gate — when muscle goes deaf.** Cortisol isn't the only catabolic signal; chronic systemic inflammation (**IL-6, TNF-α**) is an independent growth blocker. Acute, training-induced inflammation is the necessary spark for remodelling — but *chronic* inflammation (from high body fat per the [[#Link 5 — The endocrine controllers|distension ceiling]], poor sleep, or gut distress) inhibits **JAK/STAT and PI3K/Akt downstream of the IGF-1 receptor**. The androgens and GH are still circulating; the muscle simply stops hearing them. This is the same gate the body-fat ceiling reaches from the inside — gut, fat, and sleep all converge here.

> [!tip] These gates are mostly unglamorous hygiene
> Whole-food-dominant surplus, fibre, adequate (not maximal) fat, digestive support, real sleep, and staying inside the suitable body-fat range keep both gates open. They cost nothing and protect everything downstream — which is exactly why they're easy to neglect and expensive to ignore.

